# Cellar Hexagon Compiler Plugin

This plugin adds an initial Qualcomm Hexagon HAL target to IREE. It wires a
custom target backend, encoding dialect, and linker so we can lower dispatches
all the way to Hexagon-shared objects and embed them in the VMFB.

## What is included
- **HAL target backend**: `HexagonTargetBackend`/`HexagonTargetDevice` expose
  the `hexagon` target and embed executable target attributes for the HAL
  pipeline. They are also responsible for registration of the plugin and
  serialization of the executable.
- **Encoding dialect and resolver**: A lightweight `iree_hexagon` dialect
  defines `#iree_hexagon.hexagon_encoding_resolver<>`, used to carry target
  configuration to a new materializer. ExternalModels mirrors the CPU backend to
  materialize encodings and currently lower matmul-like ops to `mmt4d` with a
  simple 4x4x4 tiling prototype. This is still subject to change.
- **Pass pipelines**: For now the Hexagon pipelines delegate to the existing
  LLVMCPU pipelines (`buildLLVMCPUCodegen*`). This will change in the future.
- **Linker tool**: A custom `HexagonLinkerTool` searches for `hexagon-clang`,
  `lld`, or `ld.lld` (or uses `--iree-hexagon-linker-path`) and invokes it with
  flags that match Hexagon SDK defaults to produce ET_DYN shared objects.

## Compiler flags

Hexagon specific compiler flags added by this plugin are grouped under Hexagon HAL Target
when using: `iree-compile --help`.

Some non Hexagon specific flags that may be helpful at this point of time:
- Debug dumps: `--iree-hal-dump-executable-intermediates-to=<dir>` and
    `--iree-hal-dump-executable-binaries-to=<dir>` to capture the generated IR
    and binaries from the Hexagon backend.

- Enable tiling: `--iree-opt-data-tiling=true` to test the materializer and encoder
    from the plugin.

- Modify max vector size in LLVMCPU passes:
    `--iree-llvmcpu-max-allowed-number-of-native-vectors=2048`

## Status and next steps

This is an early prototype: it reuses CPU pipelines and a basic tiling recipe,
and Hexagon-specific vectorization or scheduling are not implemented yet. The
tests under `plugins/external/cellar-hexagon/plugins/compiler/cellar-hexagon/CodeGen/test` exercise
registration, encoding materialization, dumping, and linking to ensure the
plumbing works end to end. No unit tests have been written for already
existing LLVMCPUTarget infrastructure. Future work includes
Hexagon-tailored tiling and vectorization along making the plugin independent
from the partially reused infrastructure from the other plugin.
